  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nDry Canadian high pressure will then build across the Quad State \nregion today through Tuesday, allowing for chilly temperatures and \ndry weather for a couple of days. Highs will only be in the upper \n40s to around 50 degrees with lows in the low to mid 20s.\n\nThe next chance of rain arrives for Tuesday night before lingering \nthrough Thursday (Thanksgiving Day). The latest guidance/ensemble \ndata shows a couple pieces of upper-level energy phasing directly \nover the Quad State area Wednesday into Thursday. That makes for a \nvery challenging forecast with many solutions possible depending on \nhow those features actually phase up and there are still a few days \nto pin down the details. The latest trends have the upper low \ndeepening further and diving farther south of the region for \nThursday and evening into Friday. This would lean a bit colder \nacross the area as well. The latest EFI highlights locations just to \nthe northwest of our four-state region for an outlier type event, \nbut that could easily shift over the next day or two. \n\nReviewing the probabilistic data from multiple ensemble sources, \nshow that far northwest portions of our area, from roughly \nPerry County MO northeast through Franklin to Edwards/Wabash \nCounties and points northwest have the best chance of seeing a \ndusting to an inch of snow. In fact, there is roughly a 10% to \n30% chance of seeing more than a tenth of an inch of snow up \nthat way. The rest of the area could see some snowflakes mix in \nwith the rain. Most of the accumulation would likely remain on \ngrassy surfaces/elevated surfaces due to the recent warm \nweather. Definitely worth monitoring over the next few days!\n\nUnsettled weather may linger a bit longer as a zonal to troughed \npattern lingers nearby and shortwave energy passes through the flow. \nMoisture looks to be limited, but can't rule out some lingering \ncloud cover and possibly some light precipitation into Friday and \nSaturday before high pressure builds back into the region.",
